  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1215 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nToday - Tuesday Morning: Primarily Dry, Low Shower/Storm Potential\n\nPredominantly dry conditions are expected to continue today \nacross the Upper Mississippi River Valley. A 500hPa shortwave \ntrough across central Minnesota continues southeastward today, \nso can't completely rule out some stray showers this afternoon \nalong a surface boundary currently across northeast \nIowa/southeast Minnesota, though dry air in the 800-600hPa layer\nper RAP/HRRR soundings and mid-level water vapor imagery \nsuggest any shower activity would be dependent on the degree of \nsaturation that is able to occur and will likely be isolated in \nnature, if any can develop at all (<10%). \n\nValley fog may return again tonight into Tuesday morning under a \nsimilar set-up as last night which did result in some fog \ndevelopment within the Wisconsin and Kickapoo River valleys along \nwith some other Mississippi River tributaries. Cloud cover is the \nmain wrench in the forecast regarding fog potential as a \nthunderstorm complex is expected to develop over eastern North \nDakota/northwest Minnesota this evening, resulting in cirrus \noverspreading the area overnight. Dependent on the timing of this \ncirrus, we may be able to cool enough such that some of the \ntypically foggy river valleys see some development into the morning \nhours though increasing winds in the 500-1500ft layer may result in \nany fog development being fairly patchy. \n\nThe previously mentioned thunderstorm complex is expected to decay \nand dissipate as it translates southeastward given diminishing \ninstability, but can't rule out some isolated to scattered showers \nand perhaps a rumble of thunder (MUCAPE of 100-300 J/kg per the \n24.09z RAP) making their way into parts of southeast \nMinnesota/western Wisconsin Tuesday morning. Many CAMs do highlight \nthis potential, resulting in 10-20% probabilities per the 24.12z \nHREF. Strong/severe storms are not anticipated with this morning \nactivity. \n\nTuesday Afternoon - Evening: Strong Thunderstorm Potential\n\nGreater confidence in storms comes Tuesday afternoon and evening as \na stronger 500hPa wave currently situated along the United \nStates/Canada border across Montana/North Dakota continues eastward. \nAn associated surface low and cold front are expected to move \nthrough the region along with this upper level feature, sparking \nshowers and storms across the Upper Midwest though the greatest \npotential exists north of I-90 (30-60%) where stronger QG forcing \nand surface convergence along the front exists per the 24.14z RAP. \nThere is also an aspect of a weakening cap with northward extent.\n \nAhead of this boundary, southerly winds usher moist air into the \nregion through the day resulting in surface dewpoints climbing into \nthe low 60s and MUCAPE increasing to upwards of 1000-2000 J/kg along \nthe front. Increasing 500hPa winds associated with the approaching \nwave increases 0-6km bulk shear to 30-40kts with the vectors nearly \nperpendicular to the surface front which would suggest a \ndiscrete/supercellular storm mode and would be supportive of \nsome strong to potentially severe storms. From a hazards \nperspective, large hail followed by strong to damaging winds \nappear to be the primary concerns given the favorable \nthermodynamic and kinematic environment. There is a conditional \nthreat for a brief tornado with initial storm development given \nsome weak curvature within the 0-1km layer per model hodographs,\nbut this threat quickly wanes with time as storms begin to \nbecome elevated. \n\nWednesday - This Weekend: Mostly Dry and Near-Seasonable \n\nRidging begins to build back into the Midwest Wednesday through the \nlate week behind Tuesday night's cold front. Stability associated \nwith the ridge along with a slightly cooler and drier airmass moving \ninto the region should largely keep us dry through the late week, \nthough Wednesday may see some diurnal shower activity given \nsteepened 0-3km lapse rates from the cooler air at 850hPa and some \nlingering low level moisture. This potential is very low\nhowever, generally <10% at this point. Temperatures are \nexpected to be around 5 degrees of normal for this time of year \nthough they do tend towards the warmer side of climatology, \ngenerally topping out in the low 80s. Shower and storm potential\nlook to return this weekend.",
